Yeah, we got to meet James at NAPC when we went last year.

65
00:05:32,729 --> 00:05:34,490
Check it out if you like paleo discussions.

66
00:05:35,390 --> 00:05:35,990
NSFW.

67
00:05:37,491 --> 00:05:42,793
And we will link to a article in SciNews by Enrico de Lazaro.

68
00:05:43,254 --> 00:05:46,175
Okay, before I talk to you about sea scorpions, I'm going to talk to you about arachnids.

69
00:05:46,735 --> 00:05:47,035
Okay.

70
00:05:47,243 --> 00:05:54,347
Arachnids, spiders, scorpions, ticks, the like, were among the first arthropods to make it onto the land.

71
00:05:54,547 --> 00:05:54,727
Yes.

72
00:05:55,307 --> 00:06:00,150
But very little fossil evidence exists to show us how exactly that happened.

73
00:06:00,730 --> 00:06:06,173
Most of the earliest records of arachnid groups are already on land.

74
00:06:06,353 --> 00:06:07,374
Yeah, like established.

75
00:06:07,465 --> 00:06:12,748
Right, we don't have those ancestral points that show us that transition.

76
00:06:13,208 --> 00:06:18,811
Molecular clock estimates, which is genetic data estimating events in their evolution.

77
00:06:18,931 --> 00:06:20,072
How long things should have taken.

78
00:06:20,512 --> 00:06:24,614
Suggests that they would have become terrestrialized.

79
00:06:25,414 --> 00:06:25,935
I like that.

80
00:06:26,184 --> 00:06:33,208
Around the Cambrian Ordovician, but the first fossils don't show up until the Silurian 60 million years or so later.

81
00:06:33,588 --> 00:06:33,828
Huh.

82
00:06:34,308 --> 00:06:36,690
So we're missing some information.

83
00:06:37,190 --> 00:06:46,755
Eurypterids, the famous sea scorpions, not true scorpions, but if you Google sea scorpion, you'll see pictures of these really cool scorpion-shaped sea creatures.

84
00:06:46,775 --> 00:06:49,316
Yeah, arguably convergent in a lot of ways.

85
00:06:49,986 --> 00:06:52,727
Are considered a sister group to arachnids.

86
00:06:53,447 --> 00:06:55,468
Not true arachnids, but a close relative.

87
00:06:55,948 --> 00:07:06,613
And because they're closely related, oftentimes researchers will look to them to say, "Okay, can we understand arachnid evolution by understanding these close relatives of theirs?"

88
00:07:06,693 --> 00:07:06,913
Yes.

89
00:07:07,466 --> 00:07:15,650
There has been a couple of areas where people have suggested that maybe sea scorpions were able to move up onto land.

90
00:07:16,090 --> 00:07:23,673
For example, there are trackways of Eurypterids known that have been suggested might have been sub-aerial.

91
00:07:24,153 --> 00:07:24,573
Okay.

92
00:07:24,633 --> 00:07:25,294
Out of the water.

93
00:07:25,862 --> 00:07:37,147
And they have these unique structures on their bodies called kyemenplatten, which have been suggested to be respiratory structures that would work specifically in the air.

94
00:07:37,627 --> 00:07:37,887
Oh.

95
00:07:37,907 --> 00:07:38,847
So for air breathing.

96
00:07:39,287 --> 00:07:48,711
This research provides yet another seemingly much more definitive piece of evidence of air-breathing capability in Eurypterids.

97
00:07:49,185 --> 00:08:01,870
The discovery comes along with the identification of a new species, Adelophthalmus pyrei, which is now known from a 3D-preserved specimen, real nice specimen, from the Carboniferous of France.

98
00:08:02,450 --> 00:08:08,113
So we're looking 350 or so million years ago, which is a bit late for Eurypterids.

99
00:08:08,133 --> 00:08:09,853
They were around for quite a ways before that.

100
00:08:10,745 --> 00:08:19,510
As part of the description of this new species, the researchers were able to get CT scans of the various parts of the body, including their gills.

101
00:08:20,110 --> 00:08:24,532
The way that these gills are set up is that they are what are called book gills.

102
00:08:25,086 --> 00:08:37,691
So they have plates, basically, which I kind of, in my head, I think of sort of like the vents in your car, you know, they're plates that have gaps in between them so that water can flow through.

103
00:08:38,091 --> 00:08:39,252
The plates are called lamellae.

104
00:08:39,872 --> 00:08:45,114
On this Eurypterid, when they looked at the gills, they also saw structures called trabeculae.

105
00:08:45,524 --> 00:08:54,708
These are pillars that stretch between the plates, which we see in a lot of animals today.

106
00:08:54,828 --> 00:08:59,130
So modern-day spiders and scorpions have similar platey structures in their lungs.

107
00:08:59,150 --> 00:09:00,930
Yeah, I've heard them called book lungs.

108
00:09:00,950 --> 00:09:01,831
Book lungs, yep.

109
00:09:02,531 --> 00:09:09,214
And they will have these struts, these pillars, trabeculae, that go between the plates to prop them open.

110
00:09:10,785 --> 00:09:18,368
If you don't have this, so horseshoe crabs, for example, don't have this structure in their gills, and if they come out of water, the gills collapse.

111
00:09:18,508 --> 00:09:23,050
Yeah, because now you have surface tension holding them together.

112
00:09:23,310 --> 00:09:26,711
Right, the water can push its way through, but the air is not doing that.

113
00:09:27,852 --> 00:09:34,174
So here we have a Eurypterid with an adaptation we see in air-breathing animals today.

114
00:09:34,626 --> 00:09:41,048
An adaptation absent in horseshoe crabs who can't survive on land outside of the water, they can't breathe.

115
00:09:41,128 --> 00:09:46,610
Yeah, they can travel on land to lay eggs and stuff, but if they just hang out, they will suffocate.

116
00:09:46,650 --> 00:09:47,931
Because their gills are closed.

117
00:09:48,311 --> 00:09:51,632
But the Eurypterid, these are also clearly aquatic.

118
00:09:52,052 --> 00:09:56,374
Like this group of Eurypterids we see with paddle-shaped limbs and stuff.

119
00:09:56,865 --> 00:10:07,450
So this appears to be pretty good evidence that this is a Eurypterid that was adapted for the ability to crawl up out of the water and crawl around on land for a bit.

120
00:10:07,690 --> 00:10:10,452
Now, why they were doing this is a more open question.

121
00:10:11,006 --> 00:10:24,611
There was a note in the paper, I skimmed through the paper and there was a note in there that said that Eurypterid feeding apparati, their mouth bits, have been suggested to not work out of the water.

122
00:10:25,091 --> 00:10:25,431
Okay.

123
00:10:25,711 --> 00:10:30,173
It didn't go into detail, and the reference was to a book that I don't have access to, so I don't know why.

124
00:10:30,863 --> 00:10:37,006
But they said, hey, supposedly those might not work out of water, so they don't think it was going up there to eat.

125
00:10:37,446 --> 00:10:46,669
But it could have been going up there to lay eggs, perhaps to mate, to go into sheltered environments to produce young so that they're in a safe place.

126
00:10:47,650 --> 00:10:54,673
Which the authors point out is supported by the fact that young Eurypterids and adult Eurypterids are often found in different places.

127
00:10:56,207 --> 00:10:59,488
So they have like little nursery areas where they put the young.

128
00:11:00,149 --> 00:11:10,832
Furthermore, they point out that if the trackways are also evidence of land walking, and if those Kiehmenplaten structures are also evidence of air-breathing,

129
00:11:11,646 --> 00:11:22,189
then we see a bunch of these across different groups of Eurypterids, which might mean that air-breathing adaptations were somewhat widespread.

130
00:11:23,270 --> 00:11:31,232
And if that's the case, then they might have inherited them from an ancestor, and if they inherited air-breathing adaptations from an ancestor,

131
00:11:31,247 --> 00:11:41,711
then there's a good chance that that ancestor also gave rise to arachnids, which may also have already had air-breathing adaptations.

132
00:11:42,431 --> 00:11:52,774
So this feature, the authors point out that a) solid evidence for air-breathing in Eurypterids, very cool, b) new species, nice,

133
00:11:53,587 --> 00:12:04,811
and c) potential evidence that the ancestors of arachnids and Eurypterids were already partially capable of moving around on land.

134
00:12:05,091 --> 00:12:09,272
That these early adaptations were already showing up in their ancestors.

135
00:12:09,352 --> 00:12:14,494
Which would line up well with them being one of the earliest terrestrial invaders.

136
00:12:15,105 --> 00:12:23,028
And would also potentially fit nicely with the DNA estimates of when those features started showing up earlier in their ancestry.

137
00:12:23,268 --> 00:12:33,732
Which is neat because it means a lot like when we were talking about early tetrapods, that you could have had air-breathing features before they were doing much on land.

138
00:12:34,067 --> 00:12:42,950
Yes, if you go back to episode 77, we talked about one of the big points of that episode we discussed was moving on to land,

139
00:12:43,410 --> 00:12:51,032
and gaining the ability to move on land, and gaining the ability to survive on land, were not necessarily things that happened all at the same time.

140
00:12:51,072 --> 00:12:51,292
Yes.

141
00:12:51,813 --> 00:12:55,914
You could have evolved walk-in feet before you needed to leave the water.

142
00:12:55,923 --> 00:13:02,026
Yeah, the ability to do a push-up was handy before you were having to be on land.

143
00:13:02,367 --> 00:13:12,852
In this case, Eurypterids may have been fully aquatic, but able to breathe on land just enough so that they could crawl over to a shallow pond,

144
00:13:13,432 --> 00:13:16,053
make some babies, lay some eggs, and then crawl back.

145
00:13:16,346 --> 00:13:21,928
They weren't living on land, but they had adaptations to let them do specific functions up there.

146
00:13:22,349 --> 00:13:28,351
Possibly long before any of them fully, or their arachnid sisters, fully left the water.

147
00:13:28,571 --> 00:13:32,312
It's kind of like how there are tons of air-breathing fish today.

148
00:13:32,613 --> 00:13:33,193
Oh yeah.

149
00:13:33,644 --> 00:13:44,030
Lots of fish can breathe dry air because they live in places where either their behavior, you know, they're slow swimmers so it's useful for them to gulp it,

150
00:13:44,150 --> 00:13:50,394
or they live in a bad water with no oxygen and they need to breathe air or else they'll die.

151
00:13:50,968 --> 00:13:59,131
So there's lots of reasons you could have "land adaptations", you know, terrestrial adaptations, without being terrestrial.

152
00:13:59,351 --> 00:13:59,551
Yes.

153
00:14:00,211 --> 00:14:01,032
Cool stuff!

154
00:14:01,072 --> 00:14:02,412
It's cool, it's cool study.

155
00:14:02,432 --> 00:14:08,435
Listen, it's hard to do a Eurypterids study that's not gonna excite a little bit because they're cool animals.

156
00:14:08,695 --> 00:14:15,197
They fall on the list for me of those animals that feel like they were designed by a 10-year-old or by someone for a video game.

157
00:14:15,226 --> 00:14:19,127
They're just neat, interesting, cool-looking animals.

158
00:14:19,147 --> 00:14:20,288
Yeah, real good stuff.

159
00:14:20,748 --> 00:14:28,631
Well, staying in the ocean, my first news is about Placoderms, those armored fish that we talked about in episode 29.

160
00:14:29,071 --> 00:14:38,154
And this is a new species that potentially throws a big ol' wrench in how we thought bony skeletons evolved.

161
00:14:38,554 --> 00:14:39,155
Ooh.

162
00:14:39,625 --> 00:14:47,128
This is research by Martin Brazo et al. in Nature, Ecology, and Evolution, and the article is in The Guardian by Nicola Davis.

163
00:14:47,648 --> 00:14:52,610
So there are two main groups of fish, your bony and your cartilaginous.

164
00:14:52,810 --> 00:14:54,810
Your osteokthys and your chondrokthys.

165
00:14:54,850 --> 00:14:57,571
Yes, so bony fish are most fish.

166
00:14:57,611 --> 00:14:58,272
Think of a fish.

167
00:14:58,532 --> 00:15:03,294
Yep, if you're thinking of a fish, I would be willing to put money that it's a bony fish.

168
00:15:03,584 --> 00:15:07,686
And then the cartilaginous, which include your sharks, your rays, your chimeras.

169
00:15:08,366 --> 00:15:18,931
The common interpretation of the evolution of these groups is that the common ancestor that these branched off from had a cartilage skeleton.

170
00:15:19,352 --> 00:15:19,552
Right.

171
00:15:19,752 --> 00:15:27,115
And then they split, one group stayed cartilage, and one group developed an ossified skeleton.

172
00:15:27,165 --> 00:15:32,367
They converted that cartilage in one way or another over time into a bony skeleton.

173
00:15:32,627 --> 00:15:35,628
Right, that skeletons are derived for this group.

174
00:15:35,648 --> 00:15:35,828
Exactly.

175
00:15:35,848 --> 00:15:37,129
Something new that showed up later.

176
00:15:37,669 --> 00:15:42,952
And until now, it had been supported by the earliest jawed fish, the placoderms.

177
00:15:43,132 --> 00:15:46,413
The armored fish that include things like dunkleosteus.

178
00:15:46,545 --> 00:15:54,368
Yes, the remains of these armored fish seem to indicate that they too had cartilage skeletons.

179
00:15:54,928 --> 00:16:00,130
And so this supported that, yes, cartilage origin, then bony and sharks and stuff.

180
00:16:00,390 --> 00:16:01,130
Makes sense to me.

181
00:16:01,704 --> 00:16:14,730
This new specimen from Mongolia, which is discovered a bit back in 2012, is a 410 million year old placoderm cranium, part of the skull and the roof of the mouth.

182
00:16:15,230 --> 00:16:23,194
It is a new genus and species named Menginia turgonensis, which would have been very small, like 20 to 40 centimeters long.

183
00:16:23,614 --> 00:16:25,155
Okay, not big at all, a foot.

184
00:16:25,766 --> 00:16:31,928
And this partial roof of the skull, so they have bony armor and very often around the face.

185
00:16:32,448 --> 00:16:35,290
That's not unusual, that we knew.

186
00:16:35,770 --> 00:16:46,294
This one has what seems to be part of a braincase attached to this piece of skull that seems to be composed of bone.

187
00:16:46,775 --> 00:16:48,195
Ooh, hey, we have one of those.

188
00:16:48,447 --> 00:16:56,010
Yeah, so this now is showing a bony, at least part of the internal skeleton.

189
00:16:56,190 --> 00:16:56,650
Yeah.

190
00:16:57,110 --> 00:16:59,871
Which is not what we thought should be there.

191
00:17:01,612 --> 00:17:05,794
This is also surprising because placoderms are not a poorly understood group.

192
00:17:05,814 --> 00:17:07,115
Yeah, we've studied them a bunch.

193
00:17:07,124 --> 00:17:15,109
There's hundreds of different species, they've been studied for a long time, and none until this one had shown this kind of anatomy.

194
00:17:16,069 --> 00:17:18,050
So this now casts doubt.

195
00:17:18,571 --> 00:17:27,996
This brings into question the idea that sharks evolved from a cartilaginous ancestor and that bony skeletons were derived by bony fish.

196
00:17:28,476 --> 00:17:30,737
And there are a couple of possibilities.

197
00:17:31,385 --> 00:17:33,186
One is that bony skeletons evolved twice.

198
00:17:33,646 --> 00:17:34,286
Right.

199
00:17:34,946 --> 00:17:45,130
Yes, the ancestor to cartilaginous and bony fish was cartilage and they evolved bony skeletons and also one weird group of placoderms.

200
00:17:45,450 --> 00:17:45,650
Right.

201
00:17:45,870 --> 00:17:47,251
Way back when, separate.

202
00:17:47,863 --> 00:18:03,391
Or the more likely, the researchers suggest, possibility is that the ancestors to sharks was a bony fish and that somewhere along their evolution the chondrichthys lost the ability to produce a bony skeleton.

203
00:18:03,411 --> 00:18:13,276
